ntdll, server: completion port waits in the process with esync; latest callers for FEX.

With patch 0008 in (b154, Proton d2r7) calm play still sends about 790 remove_completion
and about as many select requests a second. D2R's file I/O threads sit in
GetQueuedCompletionStatus(Ex) on a completion port. Stock ntdll checks a port in the
process only for zero-timeout calls and only with ntsync (inproc_device_fd), so with esync
every call is a remove_completion and, for an empty queue, a select on the thread's
completion wait that the server then has to time out or wake.

The server keeps a port's sync signaled while its queue holds entries, and with esync that
sync is an eventfd the process can poll (ESYNC_MANUAL_SERVER). NtRemoveIoCompletion and
NtRemoveIoCompletionEx now wait on the port handle in the process (alertable as asked) and
send remove_completion only once the port is signaled; NtRemoveIoCompletionEx takes more
entries while the port stays signaled, without the last remove that finds it empty. A wait
that times out costs no server call. When another thread took the entry first, the rest of
the wait is the stock one (the thread's completion wait in the server, then
get_thread_completion). A port closed during the wait gives STATUS_ABANDONED_WAIT_0 as in
stock Wine. Without esync, or for a wait esync can't do, the stock path runs.

server/completion.c: a server-side waiter that takes the last entry now resets the port's
sync, and remove_completion clears it when it finds the queue empty. In stock Wine an
empty port could stay signaled after a server-side waiter took the last entry; nothing in
the process waited on that sync with esync, so it never showed.

For FEX, a second export __wine_d2r_server_calls_ext (struct d2r_server_calls_ext,
version 1, 2,112 bytes): what the in-process waits did (cp_woken, cp_timed_out,
cp_lost_race, cp_fallback, cp_extra_saved at bytes 8 to 47) and the Windows thread id of
the latest caller of each request type (last_tid[512] at byte 64). It is on with the
request counts (WINE_D2R_CALL_COUNTS). __wine_d2r_server_calls stays version 1, so FEX
builds that read it keep working. Also corrects 0008's note: a select's time is its request
only; the blocking wait comes after the reply.

Model test (eventfd port, single-threaded server model, 400,000 entries, 6 in-process and 2
stock consumers, batches of 1 to 4): no entry lost or taken twice; an idle 2 ms wait makes
no server call; without the server reset an empty port was found still signaled 107 times,
with it 0.

WINE_D2R_COMPLETION_WAIT=0 sends every completion port wait to the server as stock Wine.
Read once per process.
